---
import { renderCrsBlock } from "@crustini/syntax";
import "../styles/global.css";

const example = `+++
crustini = "0.1"
name = "Mover"
fps = 30
window = [640, 360]
+++

app! Main {
  state {
    x: number = 40;
    speed: number = 120;
  }

  fn update() {
    x += axis_x() * speed * dt();
  }

  fn draw() {
    clear(Color::Black);
    circle(x, 180, 24, Color::White);
  }
}`;

const miniExample = `rx my-sketch/main.flour
rx proof my-sketch/main.flour
rx bake my-sketch/main.flour`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content="Crustini is a single-file-first creative coding language that bakes into generated Rust." />
    <title>Crustini - a tiny .flour language</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="topbar">
      <div class="shell topbar-inner">
        <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Crustini home">
          <span class="brand-mark">rx</span>
          <span>Crustini</span>
        </a>
        <nav class="nav" aria-label="Main">
          <a href="#language">Language</a>
          <a href="#bake">Bake</a>
          <a href="/live/">Docs Live</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="shell hero-grid">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">creative coding source for playful apps</p>
            <h1>Crustini</h1>
            <p class="lede">Write immediate <code>.flour</code> sketches for little games, toys, and visual experiments. Start with Processing-style <code>draw</code>, then grow into structured <code>update</code>/<code>draw</code> apps.</p>
            <div class="actions">
              <a class="button primary" href="#bake">Bake a starter</a>
              <a class="button" href="/live/">Open Docs Live</a>
            </div>
            <div class="quick-strip" aria-label="Crustini language traits">
              <span>.flour files</span>
              <span>sketch to update/draw</span>
              <span>no Rust ceremony</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="code-frame" aria-label="Crustini source example">
            <div class="code-label">
              <span>src/main.flour</span>
              <strong>live language shape</strong>
            </div>
            <Fragment set:html={renderCrsBlock(example)} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="language" class="section section-hot">
        <div class="shell split">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">language first</p>
            <h2>Small syntax. Fast loops. Actual pixels.</h2>
            <p>Crustini keeps the first move visual: one readable <code>.flour</code> file, optional front matter, explicit state, and normal drawing calls. Small sketches can animate in <code>draw</code>; larger apps move behavior into <code>update</code>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="language-list" aria-label="Language highlights">
            <div><strong>Processing first</strong><span><code>state</code> plus <code>draw</code> is enough to make motion.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Structured when ready</strong><span><code>update</code>, <code>draw</code>, enums, matches, and helpers scale the sketch.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Rust backend</strong><span>Generated Rust stays behind the language surface.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="bake" class="section">
        <div class="shell bake-grid">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">rx bake</p>
            <h2>From starter to artifact without killing the vibe.</h2>
            <p>A bake writes generated Rust into the bakery, then Cargo builds it against <code>crustini-core</code>. Use <code>proof</code> when you only want diagnostics, and <code>bake</code> when you want the artifact.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="terminal" aria-label="Build commands">
            {miniExample.split("\n").map((line) => <code><span>$</span>{line}</code>)}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="workspace" class="section">
        <div class="shell">
          <p class="eyebrow">what is inside</p>
          <h2>The bakery is small on purpose.</h2>
          <div class="grid">
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>rx CLI</h3>
              <p><code>crates/crustini</code> owns <code>rx</code>, starters, compatibility config, bakery writing, and Cargo orchestration.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>.flour language</h3>
              <p><code>crates/crustini-lang</code> parses <code>.flour</code>, builds the app model, and emits generated Rust.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>Runtime surface</h3>
              <p><code>crates/crustini-core</code> keeps the generated app API tiny and <code>#![no_std]</code>.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>Starters</h3>
              <p>Real runnable apps live in <code>examples/</code>; compiler fixtures stay in <code>fixtures/</code>.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile accent-tile">
              <h3>Docs Live</h3>
              <p>Draft language notes with highlighted <code>flour</code> blocks and copy the preview HTML.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="tile">
              <h3>Compatibility</h3>
              <p>Current examples may keep compatibility syntax while the compiler catches up to the spec.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="footer">
      <div class="shell">MIT licensed. Version 0.0.1.</div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
